1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,215
Here's a puzzle. A bank screens every card payment with

2
00:00:03,315 --> 00:00:07,373
a fraud model. It catches ninety-five percent of fraudulent payments,

3
00:00:07,473 --> 00:00:10,689
and it wrongly flags only three percent of honest ones.

4
00:00:10,789 --> 00:00:13,823
About one payment in five hundred is actually fraud.

5
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:16,955
Now a payment gets flagged. What's the chance it really

6
00:00:17,055 --> 00:00:19,637
is fraud? Take a second and commit to a number.

7
00:00:19,737 --> 00:00:22,776
Most people say something close to ninety-five percent.

8
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:27,958
In the next few minutes you'll see why the honest answer is closer

9
00:00:28,058 --> 00:00:32,023
to six percent, and you'll meet two tools that make this kind of

10
00:00:32,123 --> 00:00:36,723
question almost mechanical: the law of total probability, and Bayes' rule.

11
00:00:37,816 --> 00:00:40,522
Forget formulas for a moment and just picture five

12
00:00:40,622 --> 00:00:43,385
hundred payments. On average, one of them is fraud.

13
00:00:43,483 --> 00:00:46,273
The screen almost always catches that one. But it

14
00:00:46,373 --> 00:00:49,104
also flags three percent of the four hundred and

15
00:00:49,204 --> 00:00:52,526
ninety-nine honest payments. That's about fifteen of them.

16
00:00:52,616 --> 00:00:55,316
So when the alarm goes off, you are looking at one

17
00:00:55,416 --> 00:00:58,900
of about sixteen flagged payments, and only one of those sixteen

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,476
is fraud. One in sixteen is about six percent.

19
00:01:01,566 --> 00:01:05,146
The screen isn't bad. The trouble is that honest payments are

20
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:08,282
so common that a small error rate, applied to all of

21
00:01:08,382 --> 00:01:12,143
them, swamps the rare fraud. Hold on to this picture. Everything

22
00:01:12,243 --> 00:01:14,736
that follows is a way of making it precise.

23
00:01:15,833 --> 00:01:19,012
To make it precise, let's trade dots for area. Draw

24
00:01:19,112 --> 00:01:22,420
every possible outcome as a square of total area one.

25
00:01:22,516 --> 00:01:25,785
Now cut the square into pieces that don't overlap and

26
00:01:25,885 --> 00:01:29,090
together fill it. A collection like that is called a

27
00:01:29,190 --> 00:01:32,395
partition. Every outcome lands in exactly one piece.

28
00:01:32,783 --> 00:01:36,688
For something concrete, think of a bank's loan book split by rating

29
00:01:36,788 --> 00:01:40,694
grade. Half the loans are grade A, thirty-five percent grade B, and

30
00:01:40,794 --> 00:01:44,699
fifteen percent grade C. Those shares are the widths of the pieces.

31
00:01:44,783 --> 00:01:48,615
Inside each grade, some fraction of loans default: one percent in

32
00:01:48,715 --> 00:01:51,580
A, four in B, twelve in C. Shade that fraction of

33
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:55,210
each column. I've stretched the heights so you can see them.

34
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:58,931
So what fraction of the whole square is shaded? Each shaded

35
00:01:59,031 --> 00:02:02,409
piece is a width times a height: the probability of the

36
00:02:02,509 --> 00:02:06,519
grade, times the probability of default given that grade. Add the

37
00:02:06,619 --> 00:02:09,175
pieces and you get point zero three seven.

38
00:02:10,266 --> 00:02:15,662
That's the law of total probability. In general, the probability of A is the

39
00:02:15,762 --> 00:02:19,783
sum, over the pieces of a partition, of P of A given B i,

40
00:02:19,883 --> 00:02:24,194
times P of B i. It's nothing more than adding up shaded area.

41
00:02:25,283 --> 00:02:28,506
Now flip the question around. A loan has defaulted. Which

42
00:02:28,606 --> 00:02:31,713
grade did it come from? Knowing that it defaulted means

43
00:02:31,813 --> 00:02:34,979
we are somewhere in the shaded region, and nowhere else.

44
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:37,559
So pull the shaded pieces out and line them up.

45
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:40,538
Together they make up point zero three seven, and each

46
00:02:40,638 --> 00:02:42,855
grade's share of that total is the answer.

47
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:46,549
Grade C is only fifteen percent of the book, but almost

48
00:02:46,649 --> 00:02:50,719
half of the defaults. And that computation is Bayes' rule: one

49
00:02:50,819 --> 00:02:54,082
shaded piece, divided by all of the shaded pieces.

50
00:02:54,166 --> 00:02:58,180
Before we saw the default, grade C had fifteen percent. After, it

51
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:02,611
has about forty-nine. The first number is called the prior, the second

52
00:03:02,711 --> 00:03:06,788
the posterior. Evidence is what carries you from one to the other.

53
00:03:07,883 --> 00:03:11,516
How much does that answer depend on how rare fraud is? Keep the

54
00:03:11,616 --> 00:03:15,724
screen exactly the same, and let the share of fraudulent payments grow.

55
00:03:15,816 --> 00:03:18,874
At two tenths of a percent we get our six percent.

56
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:22,978
At two percent fraud, a flag means about thirty-nine percent. The

57
00:03:23,078 --> 00:03:27,020
curve only crosses one half when about three percent of payments

58
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:30,240
are fraud. The base rate is doing most of the work.

59
00:03:31,333 --> 00:03:35,380
So what can the bank do? Take the sixteen flagged payments and send

60
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:39,898
them to a second, independent check: a device check that fails for ninety

61
00:03:39,998 --> 00:03:43,798
percent of fraud, and for only five percent of honest payments.

62
00:03:44,083 --> 00:03:49,001
The fraud almost surely fails again. Of the fifteen honest ones, five

63
00:03:49,101 --> 00:03:53,511
percent is less than one. So after both checks we're left with

64
00:03:53,611 --> 00:03:57,802
roughly one fraud and one honest payment. About one in two.

65
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:00,749
In exact numbers: the prior of point zero zero two

66
00:04:00,849 --> 00:04:04,052
becomes six percent after the flag, and that six percent

67
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:06,942
is the prior for the second check, which lifts it

68
00:04:07,042 --> 00:04:10,600
to fifty-three percent. Today's posterior is tomorrow's prior.

69
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:14,661
So here's the whole story in two lines. The law of

70
00:04:14,761 --> 00:04:18,580
total probability adds up the pieces of a partition. Bayes' rule

71
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,213
takes one piece and divides by all of them.

72
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:23,628
And the next time you hear that a test is

73
00:04:23,728 --> 00:04:27,598
ninety-five percent accurate, ask the question this video is really

74
00:04:27,698 --> 00:04:31,508
about: accurate, compared to what base rate? The practice problems

75
00:04:31,608 --> 00:04:34,470
for this week are on Blackboard. See you in class.

